 },
 "requested_at": "2026-11-13T20:09:09.606799Z",
 "text": "Under section 14 of Ontario’s *Residential Tenancies Act, 2006* (RTA), a provision in a tenancy agreement is void only if it prohibits \"the presence of animals in or about the residential complex.\" \n\nThis clause does not ban pets from the rental unit or the residential complex. Instead, it sets reasonable behavioural and hygiene rules for animals in shared spaces—requiring dogs to be leashed in indoor common areas and requiring tenants to pick up right after their own pets (as distinct from shifting general common-area maintenance obligations onto the tenant). Because the clause regulates pet conduct rather than prohibiting the presence of animals, section 14 does not void it, and it is a valid lease term (though any LTB remedy or eviction for breaching it remains subject to the RTA's statutory thresholds, such as substantial interference with reasonable enjoyment).\n\nVerdict: enforceable\nSection: 14",
 "finish_reason": "STOP",
